  "patterns": [
    {
      "id": "want-object",
      "pattern": "[Verb] + [Indefinite Object, accusative]",
      "exampleArabic": "أُرِيدُ قَهْوَةً",
      "exampleTransliteration": "urīdu qahwatan",
      "exampleEnglish": "I want coffee.",
      "grammarNote": "أُرِيدُ ('I want') here has a full present-tense set, same prefixes as ذهب: أُرِيدُ، تُرِيدُ (you m. / she — another instance of that homograph), تُرِيدِينَ، يُرِيدُ، نُرِيدُ. The thing wanted is the direct object of the verb, which takes the accusative case — an indefinite noun in the accusative gets a -an ending (فتحتان, tanwīn fatḥ): قَهْوَةً, not قَهْوَةٌ. You already saw this -an ending on adjectives after لَيْسَ in level 10 (لَسْتُ سَعِيدًا) — same case, different reason (direct object here vs. predicate of a negation there).",
      "audio": { "id": "phrase_ureedu_qahwatan", "text": "أُرِيدُ قَهْوَةً" },
      "introducedInLevel": 15
    }
  ],
